reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
The only chance is one die at setup assigning three rotations of the same (4,2,1) composition. Every composition is strategically equivalent up to artist relabelling, so the deal changes which artist a seat can push, not how strong the seat is. Chance on the outcome is estimated at under 10%.

When everyone plays well, the winner is decided by:
- bid shading in first-price sealed auctions;
- inferring each rival's composition, which is only 'which of two' and is resolved after one or two announced plays;
- predicting which artist will reach 3 and when, from the known hands and the 7 cards per artist;
- timing plays to avoid giving away the unpaid season-ending card;
- valuing paintings by forecasting rankings and cumulative values.
It is not decided by the die. There is real inference (hidden hands, rivals' likely next plays and bids) and a way to act on it (artist choice and bid size). A reasoning player is clearly favoured over a random bidder.
